WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:03.820
Hey Crazies.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.251
I’ve said several times that “Light doesn’t
have mass” and this has confused quite a

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.080
few of you.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.360
For most of you, the issue seems to be:
Doesn’t everything have mass?

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.720
Why wouldn’t light have it too?

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.680
Or sometimes:
Light has momentum!

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.080
Doesn’t that require mass?

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.140
All of which are fair questions, but I think
it’ll be best to start with Ashley Hughes,

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.380
who gave the question some useful context.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.640
Most of the light arriving on Earth from space

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.760
Outer Space!

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.600
comes from the Sun.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.640
You know, that big glowy ball at the center
of the solar system.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.580
In the core of that Sun, Nuclear fusion is
smashing protons together.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.780
More details on fusion in an upcoming video.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.620
Every 4 protons comes together to make 1 Helium
nucleus.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.820
But 4 protons have more mass than 1 Helium
nucleus.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:47.200
About 50 electrons worth of mass, which is
enough to worry about.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.720
Since lots of those fusion reactions are happening
inside the Sun all the time,

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.580
the Sun is losing mass at a rate of 4.3 billion
kilograms per second.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.360
That’s 3 million Jettas per second!

00:00:58.360 --> 00:00:59.600
Which might leave you wondering:

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.980
Where did all that mass go?

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:02.400
It was released as light.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.280
No, seriously.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.440
Light is like the exhaust of the Sun, yet
it powers our entire ecosystem.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:08.620
How cool is that?!

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.840
But how can mass be lost as light, if light
doesn’t have mass?!

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.100
E equals mc squared.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:13.940
It’s energy.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.500
No no, haven’t you said this?

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.900
Energy doesn't disappear or start floating around on its own

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.060
it goes into atoms, molecules, and particles.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.980
Right, that energy is contained by the photons
of light.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:26.480
But didn’t you also say this?!

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.160
There is no difference between mass and energy.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.060
Oh crap, that is a problem.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:35.640
If mass and energy are equivalent, then anything
with energy should have mass.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.900
Light has energy, so it should also have mass.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:43.820
Wait, wait, hold on a second.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.520
We need to be really careful about the word
“mass.”

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.220
We don’t want to get it all muddled up with
our preconceived notions.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.940
Hmm, let’s start with how we perceive mass.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.450
Because of General Relativity, we know light
is affected by gravity,

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:57.280
which certainly makes it look like it has
mass.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.200
Light can also create gravity.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.970
If you have enough of it, you can even create
a black hole.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:03.450
It’s called a Kugelblitz.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:06.160
But relativity basically throws mass out the
window.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.439
Gravity is the result of curved space-time
and Space-time is curved by energy, momentum,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.300
pressure, and/or stress;
not just by mass.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.220
Honestly though, because of E equals mc squared,
you could just as easily call it mass.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:19.300
Agh!

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.240
OK, we also know the more massive something is,
the less willing it is to

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:24.220
change how it’s moving.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.740
That’s what we call inertia, which we covered
in this video.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.100
Light doesn’t ever change its motion.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.860
It always travels in straight lines at a very
fast but steady speed.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:34.540
Fast! Fast!

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.600
So it has infinite inertia and, therefore,
infinite mass?!

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.160
Wait, that can’t be right.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:39.860
Hold on!

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:43.560
We know light has energy because it has to
carry something away from that nuclear reaction

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:44.520
inside the Sun.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.740
Conservation of energy shall not be violated!

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.600
Momentum is more closely related to inertia,
than energy is,

00:02:51.609 --> 00:02:54.099
but we know light has that too because it
can do this!

00:02:54.099 --> 00:02:57.269
The light collides with the blades and gives
up momentum,

00:02:57.269 --> 00:02:58.749
which makes the blades spin.

00:02:58.749 --> 00:03:02.019
So light must have momentum
and, since it can’t have an infinite amount

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.600
of momentum,
it can’t have an infinite amount of inertia

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:05.260
either.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.820
Whoa, crisis averted.

00:03:06.829 --> 00:03:08.400
But it has some though?

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.000
Well, yes and no.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.000
When you think of your own mass, you think
of all the stuff your body is made of.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.000
It’s something you have even when you’re
sitting still,

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.220
but light doesn’t ever sit still.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:18.820
Ever!

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:22.659
It’s physically impossible for light
not to go at the speed of light,

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.880
so you can’t just equate the two concepts.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.900
But... but... but.... E equals mc squared?

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.560
Okay, we’ll take a closer look at it.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.859
It’s probably the most famous equation of
all time.

00:03:33.859 --> 00:03:35.310
Except, it’s kind of a lie.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:39.029
It actually looks like this,
where the letter “p” stands for momentum

00:03:39.029 --> 00:03:41.680
from an old Latin word meaning “to go.”

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.249
If the thing isn’t moving, the equation
reduces to E equals mc squared.

00:03:46.249 --> 00:03:50.659
If instead there’s no mass, the equation
reduces to E equals pc,

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.920
which applies perfectly to light, so light
has no mass.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:59.200
Some people want E to equal mc squared so
bad, they do this,

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.780
where this “m” is a rest mass
and this “m” is called a relativistic mass.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:04.980
But that’s kinda silly.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.200
It’s much easier just to forget about mass
all together

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:09.269
and use energy and momentum instead.

00:04:09.269 --> 00:04:11.489
Theoretical physicists did it a long time
ago.

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:12.529
So, let me know in the comments.

00:04:12.529 --> 00:04:15.600
Are you satisfied with this answer?
or would you rather distinguish between rest

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.320
mass and relativistic mass?

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:18.840
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:21.300
