be a little crazy.

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:29.460
In the last video, we learned why the planets
orbit in a plane.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:30.740
Comment response time!

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.000
Jesse LaJeunesse asked if galaxies were just
really big accretion disks.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:36.300
Yep, they are.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.530
I can understand how you might think the physics
shouldn’t scale up like that.

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:42.610
But, if it makes you feel any better, that’s
about as far up as it scales.

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.740
You’ll notice galaxy clusters aren’t disks.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:48.360
The shape of the large-scale universe is controlled
by how it originally formed,

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.400
not by conservation of momentum.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.509
Nunya Bisnass was wondering why the plane
of the solar system is roughly perpendicular

00:04:54.509 --> 00:04:56.190
to the Sun’s magnetic field.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:05:00.169
As it turns out, the rotation of the Sun is
roughly parallel to the plane as well,

00:05:00.169 --> 00:05:03.300
which shouldn’t be that much of surprise since it was all rotating together to begin with.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:07.920
But the Sun’s magnetic field is generated
by convection currents in its middle layers.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.220
How those currents behave is dependent on
the direction the Sun is spinning,

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:14.920
so the magnetic field of the Sun tends to be
perpendicular to its rotation.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.020
Something that’s actually true of planets as well.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:21.710
Amy Soderstrom and Megan Gilmore were wondering
where the gravity comes from that collapses

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:23.560
the gas cloud in the first place.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.880
Anything with lots of mass will create gravity,

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.860
including a very thin gas cloud.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.080
It might not be very dense, but it’s big
like light years across big.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:37.420
It contained all the mass that will form the
Sun, the planets, and all the comets and asteroids.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.680
That’s a lot of mass no matter how dense it is,

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:42.760
so the cloud itself creates the gravity that
collapses it.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:46.940
To answer K8UR Channel’s questions: Yes,
all planetary systems are disks.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:48.240
But, no, they’re not all parallel.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.980
We’ve seen a lot of them with telescopes,
which just confirms our hypothesis on how

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:55.040
they form, but we see them
from a bunch of different angles.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.280
A lot of them have stars at their center,
but others might have multiple

00:05:58.280 --> 00:05:59.879
stars orbiting some common center of mass.

00:05:59.879 --> 00:06:01.310
It depends on the complex details.

